# DRAFT for review. Not loaded by anything: this file is outside ontology/.
# Source: spec-kit/audits/vocabulary-drift/2026-10-09.md, "Exceptions that need a Decision" and "Exceptions to re-check".
#
# Every Decision below is a draft of a choice that only the founder can make (0008-decision-records FR-002; AGENTS.md: do not guess
# a person's decision). `ifcore:decidedBy` and `ifcore:decidedAt` are left out on purpose: set them when the Decision is accepted.
# The alternatives and rejection reasons are drafted from the term's own definition in ifcore.ttl and from the sources in the audit
# report. The history of how each name was chosen is not in the repository, so each reason marked "CONFIRM" needs the founder's
# correction. Where a source was not read in full, the reason says so.
@prefix ifcore:  <https://www.intellectualfrontiers.com/ontology/core#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# ───────────────────────── 1. Native Alpha and Acquired Alpha (0019 FR-004, FR-005, FR-011) ─────────────────────────
ifcore:NativeAlphaIsTheCompanysOwnTerm a ifcore:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "Native Alpha and Acquired Alpha are the company's own terms, not restated standard terms"@en ;
    dcterms:subject ifcore:NativeAlpha, ifcore:AcquiredAlpha ;
    # ifcore:decidedBy  <set on acceptance>
    # ifcore:decidedAt  <set on acceptance>
    dcterms:description "Native Alpha originates as the founder's own authored research and thesis, independent of the company, with its own creative works and a filed trademark application (see the comment on ifcore:NativeAlpha). It is not a restatement of an established term: it requires an advantage native to a subject, carried by what the subject already has, and strong enough to change a real decision, and it separates the advantage that is native from the one the subject would have to acquire. Acquired Alpha is its counterpart for a buyer, and it counts only when the seller's evidence cost the seller something real and the buyer can check it without the seller's word. The word 'alpha' is borrowed from finance, where it means return above a benchmark; the company uses the borrowed word for an advantage above a credible benchmark, and the definitions say so. CONFIRM: that this is the intended relationship to the finance term."@en ;
    ifcore:consideredAlternative ifcore:NativeAlphaAltCompetitiveAdvantage, ifcore:NativeAlphaAltCoreCompetency, ifcore:NativeAlphaAltFinanceAlpha ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .
ifcore:NativeAlphaAltCompetitiveAdvantage a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Use 'competitive advantage' (or 'sustainable competitive advantage')"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "CONFIRM. 'Competitive advantage' names any advantage over rivals. It does not say whether the advantage is native to the subject or must be acquired, and it carries no test that the advantage changes a real decision. Those two distinctions are what the definitions of Native Alpha and Acquired Alpha add."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .
ifcore:NativeAlphaAltCoreCompetency a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Use 'core competency'"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "The scope note of Native Alpha rejects it as the unit of the claim: a core competency stated in general terms is where a search starts, not what it finds (0003-intellectual-frontiers FR-017)."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .
ifcore:NativeAlphaAltFinanceAlpha a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Use 'alpha' alone, in its finance meaning (return above a benchmark)"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "CONFIRM. In finance, alpha is measured against a market benchmark. Native Alpha is claimed for a person, team, product, dataset or set of relationships and is traced to its source, and an unusual and disproportionate advantage is required. 'Native' is what separates it from the advantage that must be acquired."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .

# ───────────────────────── 2. IPLG (0019 FR-004, FR-010) ─────────────────────────
ifcore:IPLGBuildsOnProductLedGrowth a ifcore:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "IPLG keeps its name and is stated as a specialization of product-led growth"@en ;
    dcterms:subject ifcore:IPLG ;
    # ifcore:decidedBy  <set on acceptance>
    # ifcore:decidedAt  <set on acceptance>
    dcterms:description "'Product-led growth' is an established go-to-market term: the product itself drives acquisition, activation and retention. IPLG is not a new idea beside it. It applies the same idea to a unit's intellectual output (a paper, a tool, a book's executable content) and adds one test: one useful action completed before any commercial ask. The draft outcome is to keep the name 'Intellectual Product-Led Growth', to write the full name first and the acronym second in every definition, and to state in the definition that it specializes product-led growth. Source for the established term: vendor and analyst pages only; no standard defines it, and its originator was not verified (see the audit report). CONFIRM: that the added test is the part that is the company's own."@en ;
    ifcore:consideredAlternative ifcore:IPLGAltPlainPLG, ifcore:IPLGAltFreeTrial ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .
ifcore:IPLGAltPlainPLG a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Use 'product-led growth' with no new term"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "CONFIRM. The established term describes a company's software product. It does not cover research, a book or a tool offered as an intellectual product, and it has no rule that the visitor completes one useful action before any commercial ask. Kept as a specialization, not a replacement."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .
ifcore:IPLGAltFreeTrial a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Use 'freemium' or 'free trial'"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "CONFIRM. Those name a pricing offer. IPLG names the visitor's first useful action, which can occur with no account and no price."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .

# ───────────────────────── 3. Alternatives to add to existing Decisions (0019 FR-004; 0008 FR-006) ─────────────────────────
# These add one considered alternative to a Decision already in ifcore.ttl. Because an accepted Decision is a record (0008 FR-008,
# FR-009), the founder chooses: (a) add the alternative to the existing Decision, or (b) record a new Decision that supersedes it.
# The draft assumes (a). It changes no outcome.

# Eidolon: 'digital twin' (ISO 23247 is the manufacturing standard for it).
ifcore:EidolonErgonSeparation ifcore:consideredAlternative ifcore:EidolonAltDigitalTwin .
ifcore:EidolonAltDigitalTwin a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Call an Eidolon a 'digital twin'"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "CONFIRM. ISO 23247 (manufacturing) defines a digital twin of an observable manufacturing element (personnel, equipment, materials, processes, facilities, products) that monitors the element with continuously updated data and can control it; only the abstract was read, as the text is paid. An Eidolon reflects any existing subject, including a person, a company or a relationship, carries an audience and a source on every fact, records state and what changed, and does not control its subject. The term 'digital twin of an organization' is also in use outside ISO 23247; that use was not checked. If the founder holds that an Eidolon is a digital twin, the better outcome is to keep 'Eidolon' as the company's name and map it with skos:closeMatch to a digital-twin concept."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .

# Noema, and Ergon: borrowed words, not invented ones (0019 FR-011).
ifcore:NoemaThirdKind ifcore:consideredAlternative ifcore:NoemaAltBorrowedWord .
ifcore:NoemaAltBorrowedWord a ifcore:Alternative ; rdfs:label "Treat 'Noema' and 'Ergon' as original coinages"@en ;
    ifcore:rejectionReason "CONFIRM. 'Noema' is an established word in philosophy (Husserl's term for what a thought is directed at) and 'ergon' is classical Greek for 'work'; neither is invented. They are borrowed and given one exact meaning here. 0019 FR-011 says a borrowed word is not original for that reason, so the exception rests on the kinds being the company's own classification (0047-digital-reflections, 0048-noemas) and not on the words. This origin was stated from general knowledge and was not searched in this audit."@en ;
    ifcore:hasAudience ifcore:Public .
